  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain will continue today, then colder air \nwill produce scattered wet snow and rain showers tonight through the \nweekend with minor accumulations possible across higher terrain.\n\nSurface low pressure over the Delmarva peninsula this afternoon will \nmove off the east coast by this evening. A long wave trough \nwith multiple embedded shortwave troughs will move across the \neastern CONUS through the start of the weekend.\n\nRegional radar shows widespread precipitation across the \nforecast area this afternoon. A narrow band of rain, with snow \nacross the highest spots of the Tug Hill, stretches from the \nLake Erie shoreline to the western Adirondacks. Additionally, \nrain will continue across the western Southern Tier into the \nFinger Lakes region. Rainfall amounts of less than 0.10\" have\nbeen observed across the Niagara Frontier and Saint Lawrence \nValley, to 0.10-0.25\" elsewhere.\n\nThe low level flow will slowly back across the region as the \nsurface low moves off the east coast tonight. An area of \nfrontogenesis exists north of the surface low, with focused \nareas of rain across the eastern Great Lakes region. Evaporative\ncooling across the Tug Hill region, led to an early change over\nto snow with a light coating visible on the Harrisburg NYSM \ncamera. A shortwave trough will move across the region tonight, \nwith moisture pulling away from the region. Thermal profiles \nwill cool and exiting rain across the Finger Lakes and western \nSouthern Tier will transition to snow overnight. A coating is \npossible across the higher terrain of the Finger Lakes region. \nUpslope and lake enhancement snow showers will develop late \ntonight across the Chautauqua Ridge and a coating is possible by\ndaybreak Friday. \n\nThe set-up will become favorable for upslope and lake effect \nprecipitation Friday, however synoptic moisture appears to be \nlacking for any organized precipitation. Cold air advection and a \nlow freezing height will support rain changing to snow at times. \nSnow is expected across the higher terrain of western NY, and a \ncoating to 2\" are expected along the Chautauqua Ridge and Bristol \nHills. \n\nA sharp mid-level trough axis is expected to swing through the \nregion Saturday. This will support an uptick in precipitation \nacross the region, however it will be short-lived. A quick \ncoating is possible, especially across the higher terrain. \nOverall, snowfall accumulations will be minor through Saturday \nnight, with 1-3\" possible across the higher terrain of the Tug \nHill (mainly through tonight,) and the Chautauqua Ridge and \nBristol Hills. Little or no accumulation is expected for lower \nelevations.\n\nA 500 hPa subtle trough axis within the departing large scale \ndeparting mid level low will drop across our Sunday. Much drier air \nbehind this feature will expand through the snow growth zone Sunday \nmorning, with any lingering lake enhanced showers of snow or rain \nand upslope activity on a northwest flow ending through the morning \nhours. A few showers may linger across CNY into the early afternoon \nhours. The still cool northwest flow and shallow moisture will \nmaintain a wealth of cloud cover through the afternoon and overnight \ntime period. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with \ntemperatures trending warmer by midweek.\n\nAn expansive area of surface high will build from the Plains and \nacross the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley early next week. This feature \nwill bring a return to dry conditions, with perhaps a few partly to \nmostly sunny days. Temperatures will remain cool ahead of the \nsurface high, but as this surface high slides eastward, and a \nstronger southerly flow ensues, afternoon temperatures will rise \nback into the lower 50s by Tuesday or Wednesday of next week...with \nthe warming trend to well above normal temperatures continuing into \nThursday.",
